Quoting  Michal Brzozowski  <rusolis@poczta.fm>:
>1. Work on the current codebase, adding new features and fixing
>things, basically continuing what slashem and sporkhack had started.
>2. Break Nethack into pieces and assemble it into a modern OS project,
>not only code wise, but in general.  By that I mean community
>oriented, with a bugtracker, repository, beta releases, etc.  Code
>wise that would mean translating into a modern programming language
>like C++, Java, Python, D, etc (most likely object-oriented).

You really have three things here:

1) Work on the current codebase.
2) Have a more open development process.
3) Rewrite in another language.

You have rolled 2) and 3) into one, but they are not particularly related
to each other.

1) seems like an obvious way to show immediate rewards. Grab fixes for the
known bugs from Spork, fix the rest yourself, start thinking about
features and balance issues (hem-hem you could do worse than steal from
Spork).

2) is not a bad idea, but you must beware democracy of design. Whatever
the development process is, you cannot leave game design issues down to
majority vote without producing a bland mish-mash. A single person or a
small core team is needed to produce a flavourful design - not everyone
will _like_ that flavour, but at least you'll _have_ a flavour.

3) is a pipe dream. Even if you get past the struggle over everyone's
favourite toy language and make the sensible decision to write in C++ (I
know I'm going to get a kicking for this one, but the advantage in terms
of similarity to the language of the existing code is compelling - and the
only other realistic alternative, Java, makes terminal output a
nightmare), you're looking at a colossal amount of work for no immediate
gain.

Sure, it _might_ make future development easier, but what you're trying
to motivate people to do is a vast amount of work during which - well, if
you're well-organised you'll have something that compiles and isn't
interesting, and if you're not you'll be desperately hoping that at some
point in the future when you've rewritten another few thousand lines of
source you'll be able to compile it again (which you won't, and your
project will then implode) - and what will you have at the end of this
epic struggle? NetHack 3.4.3. We've _got_ NetHack 3.4.3. Who's going to
get excited about turning out NetHack 3.4.3 because at some ill-defined
point in the future you might be able to start doing the same sorts of
things Derek Ray was doing in 2007?

There may well be another official Dev Team release at some point (I still
think that is more likely than not) at which point your porting project
(which will not have finished because it will never finish) will be lost
without trace as you try to port a gigantic patch into your half-finished
mess.

Conversely we know already that a well-crafted patch (like the Wizard
Patch) can make it into the official releases - but that's only going to
happen if it's a patch _to_ the existing code. If you want the mantle
passed from the (_hypothetically_) burned-out Dev Team, a solid 3.4.3
bugfix release and then solid well-considered balance and feature
improvements is your best shot; if you want NAO to consider replacing the
official 3.4.3 with a third-party bugfix and/or offering a feature
release, that approach also strikes me as your best shot.

Learning C and programming in C - particularly in the environment of the
NetHack source, where all that ugly systems programming stuff is already
done - hasn't got any harder over the years than it ever was (and,
contrary to popular belief, "object orientation" is not a magic pixie dust
you can sprinkle on yourself to make programming ten times easier); and
it's the only way to embark on a project that will show immediate rewards
and get people enthused about your ideas. 

A rewrite will take - well, optimistically, you might merely think
"several months"; I don't expect you to believe me that it will never
finish, but I'm right - and show no reward at all until you are finished.



